import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/AdminPreview
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The admin package: what it is for, installing it, a first admin (code the site's server
## compiles), its pages as the package renders them (from made-up tables, while this page renders),
## its URLs, filters, access, options and widgets, how it works, the jobs page, its modules, and what
## it doesn't do yet. All of it is static: the browser runs nothing here.
meta = {
	title: "Admin",
	description: "A Django-style admin for a Roc Solid app's tables, on Postgres or SQLite: each table's rows with a search, a filter in every column, sorting and pages, and a form for each row, read from the database's own catalog.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "first", label: "A first admin" },
		{ id: "looks", label: "What it looks like" },
		{ id: "pages", label: "Its pages" },
		{ id: "filters", label: "Filters" },
		{ id: "access", label: "Who may use it" },
		{ id: "options", label: "Options" },
		{ id: "widgets", label: "Widgets" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "jobs-page", label: "The jobs page" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/admin</code>]}
				title="An admin for your tables, read from the database itself"
			>
				A Django-style admin for your app's database. Each table gets a page of its rows, with a search,
				a filter in every column, sorting and pages, and a form to add, change or delete a row. The admin
				reads your tables from the database's own catalog (Postgres's or SQLite's) on each request, so
				there is no code to generate and nothing to keep in step with your schema. Use it when you want to
				see and fix your data without building pages for it.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				You mount it with one route file, behind your app's own middleware. The package also draws
				the <a href={"#jobs-page"}>jobs page</a>, a plugin that shows the queue of
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>packages/jobs</a>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header (<code>main.roc</code>), by its directory's name. This is
				how the docs site names it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.admin_header.file} source={Sources.admin_header.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The package names the server platform (for the jobs page), so its <code>pf</code> must be the same
				platform file your app names. The admin runs only on the server: the browser's half of your app
				never compiles it, since nothing in the browser imports a <code>+server.roc</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="first">A first admin</Heading>
			<p>
				Add a route file at the path the admin should have: <code>routes/admin/db/+server.roc</code> is
				<code>/admin/db</code>. Its <code>respond!</code> hands every request to
				<code>AdminPage.respond!</code>, with three more things:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					the context the route's <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> handed on
					(<code>middleware = Middlewares.staff</code> says who is asking);
				</li>
				<li>
					the admin's options: only <code>mount</code> (the route's path) and <code>access</code> are
					needed, and for each table only its <code>name</code>;
				</li>
				<li>the effects the admin runs on: <code>Db.all!</code>, <code>Db.run!</code>, <code>Random.bytes!</code> and <code>Db.backend!</code>.</li>
			</ul>
			<Code title={Sources.admin_route.file} source={Sources.admin_route.rocx} />
			<p>
				In your app these definitions go in the route file. On this site they are a module, so the
				compiler checks them with the site's server, but nothing answers with them: the docs site has no
				database.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Why you hand it Db.all!">
				<p>
					<code>AdminPage</code> uses no platform, so a page in either half of an app can import the
					admin's modules (this page shows the admin's own pages that way, below). A module on no platform
					can't call <code>Db</code>, so your route passes it the server platform's effects, and the compiler
					checks that they fit. <code>Db.backend!</code> tells the admin whether your database is Postgres or
					SQLite, so it writes its statements for that one.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="looks">What it looks like</Heading>
			<p>
				These are the admin's pages as the package's own <code>View</code> renders them while this page
				renders. They come from a made-up catalog of two tables, <code>books</code> and <code>authors</code>,
				with the options above. Nothing asks a database, and the frames are sandboxed, so their links and
				forms do nothing here. They are drawn in the admin look, the same as the
				<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_jobs}#page"}>jobs page</a>: the tables in a sidebar (across the top when the
				frame is narrow), a header with the page's actions, panels, and light or dark to match your system.
			</p>
			<figure class="admin-preview">
				<iframe class="list" title="The admin's list of books, as the package renders it" srcdoc={AdminPreview.list_page} sandbox="" loading="lazy"></iframe>
				<figcaption>
					A table's rows, filtered to a <code>price</code> of 10 or more. Under each heading is a filter
					chosen from the column's type: a range for a number, the text a title contains, the value of a
					foreign key, and a select of the check's three values for <code>state</code>. Enter or the Filter
					button sends them. A heading sorts by its column, the search looks in <code>title</code> and
					<code>blurb</code>, and a foreign key links to its row.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<figure class="admin-preview">
				<iframe title="The admin's form for one book, as the package renders it" srcdoc={AdminPreview.row_page} sandbox="" loading="lazy"></iframe>
				<figcaption>
					A row's form, just saved: a select of authors for the foreign key, a select of the check's values
					for <code>state</code>, a checkbox, a decimal's input, a moment in UTC, and a text area for text of
					several lines. The key is shown, not editable.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<details>
				<summary>The made-up tables, and the pages rendered from them: <code>{Sources.admin_preview.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.admin_preview.file} source={Sources.admin_preview.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="pages">Its pages</Heading>
			<p>
				Every page is the admin's path plus a query, which is why one route file is enough. A query can
				name only the tables and columns the app lets the admin show.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Query</th><th>Page</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>(none)</td><td>the tables, each with roughly how many rows it has (Postgres's estimate; SQLite gives none), its comment, and a link to add a row</td></tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>?table=books</code></td>
							<td>
								its rows, 50 a page (<code>page=2</code>), counted and paged by the database; a search
								(<code>q=cloud</code>), a filter in each column (<code>has.title=cloud</code>,
								<code>from.price=10</code>, <code>f.state=draft</code>, <code>is.author_id=null</code>: see
								<a href="#filters">Filters</a>), and a sort (<code>sort=title</code>, or
								<code>sort=-price</code> for descending)
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr><td><code>?table=books&amp;k.id=21</code></td><td>a row's form: <code>k.</code> plus a column for each column of its primary key, so a key of two columns works too</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>?table=books&amp;new</code></td><td>a new row's form, with its constant defaults filled in (<code>true</code>, <code>'draft'</code>, <code>0</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>?table=books&amp;k.id=21&amp;delete</code></td><td>the question before a row is deleted, and the button that deletes it</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Sending a form adds, changes or deletes the row, then takes you to the row's page, which says
				Saved. An update writes only the columns that changed. When the database refuses (a price that
				isn't a number, an author that books still name), the form comes back with what you sent and the
				database's reason. A table without a primary key is listed, but its rows can't be opened.
			</p>

			<Heading id="filters">Filters</Heading>
			<p>
				Each column a list shows has a filter, in a row under the column headings, chosen from the
				column's type. A hidden column never has one. There is no option to turn filters on or off:
				filtering a column the list already shows reveals nothing a visitor can't see. To filter by a
				column, list it.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Filter</th><th>Query</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>text</code>, <code>varchar</code>, <code>uuid</code>, <code>json</code> and any other type</td><td>the text it contains, in any case; <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> match themselves</td><td><code>has.title=cloud</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>a number: <code>integer</code>, <code>bigint</code>, <code>numeric</code>, <code>real</code>, …</td><td>from, to, or both, inclusive</td><td><code>from.price=10&amp;to.price=20</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>date</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, <code>timestamptz</code> (in UTC), <code>time</code></td><td>from, to, or both, inclusive, with the browser's pickers</td><td><code>from.published_at=2026-03-01T00:00</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>a select: any, yes, no</td><td><code>f.in_print=true</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>an enum, or a check that it is one of a few values</td><td>a select of its values</td><td><code>f.state=draft</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>a foreign key</td><td>the value it equals</td><td><code>f.author_id=3</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>any of these, if it may be null</td><td>also empty and not empty: in its select, or in a small select under its inputs</td><td><code>is.blurb=null</code>, <code>is.blurb=set</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The filters are one GET form, with no JavaScript. Enter in any of them sends it, and so does the
				Filter button beside the search. Filtering keeps the search and the sort and goes back to the first
				page; the page links and the sort keep the filters, and Clear filters drops them all.
			</p>
			<p>
				Each filter's value is checked in Roc before it reaches SQL: a number for a number,
				<code>2026-03-14</code> for a date, one of an enum's labels, and so on. A range's value is then cast
				to its column's type (<code>{"\"price\" >= $3::numeric"}</code>). A value of the wrong kind is ignored,
				and its filter says so (Ignored: not a number), so a bad query never turns the page into an error.
			</p>

			<Heading id="access">Who may use it</Heading>
			<p>
				The route's middleware runs first, as for any route, so it can send a stranger to sign in. Then
				<code>access</code> decides from the context the middleware handed on:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th><code>access</code> says</th><th>Then</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Edit</code></td><td>every page, and adding, changing and deleting rows</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ReadOnly</code></td><td>the tables, rows and values; no forms</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Refused</code></td><td>a 403, and nothing is read</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A form's POST must come from a page of the same site (its <code>Origin</code> header) and send back
				the token its page was given. The admin keeps that token in a cookie,
				<code>rocx_admin_csrf</code>, which is sent only to the admin's path, can't be read by scripts
				(<code>HttpOnly</code>), and isn't sent with another site's form (<code>SameSite=Lax</code>). Any
				other POST is refused with a 403 before a table is read.
			</p>

			<Heading id="options">Options</Heading>
			<p><code>Admin.Config(context)</code> configures the admin. Only <code>mount</code> and <code>access</code> are required.</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>mount : Str</code></td><td>the route's path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"access : context -> [Edit, ReadOnly, Refused]"}</code></td><td>what someone may do, decided from the middleware's context</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>tables : List(Admin.Table)</code></td><td>the tables it shows, in order, with their options (none by default)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>include : [Listed, All]</code></td><td><code>Listed</code> (the default) shows only those tables; <code>All</code> adds every other table of the schema, with default options, except the platform's own (<code>rocx_…</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>schema : Str</code></td><td>the Postgres schema: <code>"public"</code> by default (SQLite's is always <code>main</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>what its pages call it: <code>"Database"</code> by default</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"back : { label : Str, href : Str }"}</code></td><td>a link back to the app's own pages, first in every page's sidebar (<code>{"{ label: \"Status admin\", href: \"/admin\" }"}</code>); none by default</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Admin.Table</code> holds a table's options. Columns are named as the table names them. A table
				or a column the schema doesn't have stops the admin: its pages say what is wrong and show nothing
				else until it is mended, since a misspelt <code>hidden</code> would show the column it was meant to
				hide.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td>(required)</td><td>the table</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>label : Str</code></td><td>its name, made readable: <code>book_tags</code> is Book tags</td><td>what the pages call it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>list : List(Str)</code></td><td>its first seven columns</td><td>the columns its list of rows shows, each with a filter</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>search : List(Str)</code></td><td>no search box</td><td>where the search looks, for the text anywhere, in any case</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ordering : List(Str)</code></td><td>its key, newest first</td><td>the list's order; <code>-</code> for descending: <code>["-published_at", "title"]</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>read_only : List(Str)</code></td><td>none</td><td>columns shown on the form, never written</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>hidden : List(Str)</code></td><td>none</td><td>columns never read, shown or written, such as a password's hash</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>labels : List((Str, Str))</code></td><td>each column's name, made readable</td><td>what to call its columns: <code>[("email", "Email address")]</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>its first text column that isn't its key</td><td>the column that names a row: the heading of its page, and its name in a foreign key's select</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>per_page : U64</code></td><td>50</td><td>rows a page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>widgets : List((Str, Widget.Kind))</code></td><td>the column's type's</td><td>another input for a column: <code>[("body", LongText)]</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>create : Bool</code>, <code>delete : Bool</code></td><td>yes</td><td>whether rows may be added, or deleted, here</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="widgets">Widgets</Heading>
			<p>
				A widget is the input a column gets on a row's form. The admin chooses it from the column's type,
				as the catalog reports it (for a domain, its base type):
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Widget</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>a foreign key to a table the admin shows</td><td>a select of that table's rows, by their title (an input, when there are more than 200), and a link to the row</td></tr>
						<tr><td>an enum, or a check that it is one of a few values (<code>{"check (state in ('draft', 'published'))"}</code>)</td><td>a select of them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>a checkbox; a select of yes, no and null when it may be null</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smallint</code>, <code>integer</code>, <code>bigint</code></td><td>a number</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>numeric</code>, <code>real</code>, <code>double precision</code></td><td>a decimal's input</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>date</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, <code>timestamptz</code>, <code>time</code></td><td>the browser's pickers; a <code>timestamptz</code> in UTC</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>json</code>, <code>jsonb</code></td><td>a text area, in a monospace font</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>text</code>, <code>varchar(n)</code>, <code>uuid</code> and the rest</td><td>an input (with <code>maxlength</code> for a <code>varchar(n)</code>), or a text area for a value of several lines; the database reads what is typed as the column's type</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a generated column, <code>bytea</code></td><td>shown, never written</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				An empty input means null for a column that may be null, and an empty text for a text column that
				may not. In a new row, an empty input for a column with a default leaves the default to the
				database. To give a column another widget, name it in the table's <code>widgets</code>; the
				<a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/blob/master/packages/admin/README.md#widgets">README</a>
				lists every <code>Widget.Kind</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				On each request, the admin reads the schema from the database's catalog and turns the answer into
				tables (<code>Catalog.read</code>): their columns and types, which columns may be null or have
				defaults, the primary keys, foreign keys, enums, and the values a check allows. On Postgres that is
				four small statements over <code>pg_catalog</code>. On SQLite it reads
				<code>pragma_table_list</code>, <code>pragma_table_xinfo</code> and
				<code>pragma_foreign_key_list</code>, and takes each table's checks from its
				<code>create table</code>. A SQLite column's declared type is read as Postgres would name it
				(<code>integer</code> as <code>int8</code>, <code>boolean</code> as <code>bool</code>), so it gets the
				same widget and filter.
			</p>
			<p>
				A table or column that a request names is looked up in those tables, among the ones the app lets
				the admin show. Only the catalog's names go into SQL, double-quoted, and every value is a
				parameter:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.admin_ident.file}, in part"} source={Sources.admin_ident.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A list is two statements, built by <code>Sql</code>: how many rows there are, then the page's rows.
				These are the statements for <code>{"?${AdminPreview.query}"}</code> in the admin above, built by the
				package while this page renders:
			</p>
			<Code title={"?${AdminPreview.query}"} source={AdminPreview.statements} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Every statement answers with rows of one shape, each cell as text plus whether it was null
				(<code>Catalog.Row</code>), so one <code>Db.all!</code> reads them all, whatever the table. A cell in a
				list is cut short by the database (to 81 characters, so the page can tell it was cut); a key never
				is.
			</p>
			<p>
				Pages are sent with <code>Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'</code> (no script, nothing
				loaded from elsewhere, forms sent only to this site), <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code> and
				<code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>, and every value in them is escaped.
			</p>

			<Heading id="jobs-page">The jobs page</Heading>
			<p>
				The package also draws the queue of <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>packages/jobs</a>, as a plugin.
				You list it beside the jobs plugin, with a <code>guard</code>: the middleware that decides who may
				see it, usually the one your admin pages use. <code>guard</code> has no default, so a jobs page is
				never public because an option was left out.
			</p>
			<Code source={"plugins: [\n\tJob.plugin({ jobs: App.jobs }),\n\tJobsPage.plugin({ at: \"/admin/jobs\", guard: Middlewares.admin }),   # at: \"/admin/jobs\" unless it says\n]"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A request at <code>at</code> passes the app's middleware, then <code>guard</code>, then the page; what
				the guard hands on isn't used. The page needs the jobs plugin, and the server says so at start if it
				isn't listed. <code>back</code> adds a link back to your app's pages, as the tables admin's option does
				(<code>{"JobsPage.plugin({ guard, back: { label: \"Status admin\", href: \"/admin\" } })"}</code>). To
				mount the page yourself, answer a route with <code>{"JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount })"}</code>
				(<code>back</code> is optional there too). The jobs page's own section says
				<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_jobs}#page"}>what it shows and does</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				The tables admin, the jobs page and the dev mailbox of <a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>packages/email</a>
				share one look. <code>Look</code> has the rules every admin page shares and the helpers that draw
				them, and <code>AdminHtml</code> builds the server's pages on them.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>AdminPage</code> is the one module with effects, and they are the ones your route hands it.
				<code>JobsPage</code> and <code>AdminHtml</code> run on the server platform. The rest is plain Roc,
				on no platform: <code>roc test packages/admin/main.roc</code> runs their <code>expect</code>s.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Admin</code></td><td>the options (<code>Config</code>, <code>Table</code>, <code>Access</code>), what they name that the schema doesn't have (<code>problems</code>), and what they mean for a table: <code>showing</code>, <code>listed</code>, <code>searchable</code>, <code>writable</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>ordering</code>, <code>widget</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>AdminPage</code></td><td><code>respond!(request, context, config, effects)</code>: the access check, the token, the catalog, then the page or the form</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Catalog</code></td><td>the statements that read the schema, on Postgres or SQLite, and <code>read</code> (<code>read_sqlite</code>), their rows as tables</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Sql</code></td><td><code>ident</code>, <code>contains</code> (a search's or a filter's pattern), the conditions a list's rows meet, and the statements: <code>rows</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>one</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>titles</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Widget</code></td><td>a column's widget from its type (<code>of</code>), its input (<code>input</code>), what a form's text writes (<code>param</code>), whether it changed (<code>same</code>), a new row's value (<code>initial</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Listing</code></td><td>a list's search, filters, sort and page, read from the query and written back (<code>read</code>, <code>params</code>, <code>conditions</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>key</code>); each column's filter (<code>kind</code>) and the check of its values (<code>check</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Paging</code></td><td><code>of(total, size, asked)</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>shows</code>, <code>links</code>: the first two pages, three each side of this one, the last two</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>View</code></td><td>each page's HTML: <code>index_page</code>, <code>list_page</code>, <code>row_page</code>, <code>delete_page</code>, <code>problem_page</code>, <code>problems_page</code>, and the sidebar (<code>side</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Look</code></td><td>the admin look: the rules every admin page shares (<code>shared</code>) and its helpers (<code>side</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>panel</code>, <code>empty</code>, <code>icon</code>, <code>button_with</code>), light and dark; the tables admin's own rules, its document, and the headers it is sent with</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Form</code></td><td>a query's or a form's fields, decoded, and links, encoded</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>JobsPage</code></td><td>the jobs page: <code>{"plugin({ at, guard, back })"}</code>, or <code>{"respond!(request, { mount, back })"}</code> for an app that mounts it itself (server only)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>AdminHtml</code></td><td>the server's admin pages (the jobs page, the dev mailbox), on <code>Look</code>: a page with its stream of changes, times, links (server only)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<p>
				Everything above works today, on Postgres and on SQLite. The server's tests drive the admin over
				HTTP on both: its index, a new row's form, a row created, found by a search and a filter, changed
				and deleted.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>A list counts its rows (<code>count(*)</code>) and pages with <code>offset</code>: fine for thousands of rows, slow for millions.</li>
				<li>A filter on the text a column contains reads every row (no index helps <code>{"ilike '%…%'"}</code>), as the search does.</li>
				<li>A foreign key in a list shows its value, linked to its row, not the row's title; its filter takes a value.</li>
				<li>A foreign key or a check over several columns gives no widget: each column is edited alone.</li>
				<li>No actions on several rows at once, and no history of who changed what.</li>
				<li>
					Every route passes the effects. <code>AdminPage</code> could take <code>Db</code> itself, in a
					server-only module, and a route would be <code>AdminPage.respond!(request, context, admin)</code>;
					the package's <code>PROPOSALS.md</code> weighs it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				The <a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/tree/master/packages/admin">package's README</a> is
				the full reference, and its proposals are beside it in <code>packages/admin/</code>. The pages above
				are drawn by its <code>View</code>, and the code is its own.
			</Note>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* The admin's own pages, in frames: as wide as the text, which is narrow enough that the admin
	   lists its tables across the top, as on a phone. A book's form is taller than its frame, and
	   scrolls in it. */
	.admin-preview {
		margin: var(--space-5) 0;
	}

	.admin-preview iframe {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: 60rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: #f4f6f7;
	}

	/* The list of books, whole: its filters in its table, its six rows and its count; on a phone,
	   its titles take two lines. */
	.admin-preview iframe.list {
		height: 52rem;
	}

	@media (max-width: 40rem) {
		.admin-preview iframe.list {
			height: 57rem;
		}
	}

	.admin-preview figcaption {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: var(--size-sm);
	}
</style>
